commit faeff03649fa2214694afc5b93e9bd7c3d132ebe
Author: Alex Yang <agegcn@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 30 22:25:56 2026 +0800

    fix(chat): keep typed line breaks in user messages (#1015)
    
    * fix(chat): keep typed line breaks in user messages (#680)
    
    User messages are rendered as Markdown, and
    `.markdown-user-message p { white-space: pre-wrap }` keeps single line
    breaks inside paragraphs. Several cases still collapsed typed line
    breaks into spaces:
    
    - Lone `\r` line endings: Chrome renders `\r` as a space even under
      pre-wrap, which merges pasted text into one paragraph exactly as
      reported.
    - Soft breaks inside tight list items (an exam question
      `1. question\nA. option\nB. option`) and setext headings. Their text is
      not inside a `<p>`, and adding pre-wrap to `li` turns the newlines
      between block children into blank lines.
    - Hard breaks (two trailing spaces or `\`) showed an extra blank line,
      because remark-rehype emits `<br>` plus a "\n" that pre-wrap renders
      again.
    
    User messages now go through a small built-in remark plugin, selected by
    a new `keepLineBreaks` prop on MarkdownBody, that turns every line ending
    in text into a bare `<br>` and uses the same `<br>` for hard breaks.
    Code, inline code, math and raw HTML keep their text. UserMessageView
    also normalizes `\r\n`/`\r` so the plain-text paths (skill-command args,
    the raw view of oversized messages) keep their breaks. Assistant
    rendering is unchanged.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(chat): leave blocks with raw-text tags to the default rendering (#680)
    
    A user message that mentions `<textarea>`, `<title>` or `<script>` without
    backticks opens a raw-text element in rehype-raw, and the <br> the line-break
    plugin placed after it ended that state: the lines were glued together or
    the rest of the paragraph disappeared. A paragraph, heading or table cell
    holding such a tag now renders as it did before, where the pre-wrap rule
    still shows its newlines.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
